1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,961
Alex: Here is a small story. One person changes a rule for his A I assistants. Twice.

2
00:00:06,961 --> 00:00:08,267
Alex: In one morning.

3
00:00:08,842 --> 00:00:13,009
Maya: And each time, he adds the new rule to the memory file.

4
00:00:13,638 --> 00:00:15,638
Alex: But he never deletes the old one.

5
00:00:16,243 --> 00:00:19,349
Maya: So now the file says two different things.

6
00:00:19,349 --> 00:00:23,232
Maya: And the assistant just believes whichever line it reads first.

7
00:00:23,727 --> 00:00:27,946
Alex: Today we talk about why stale memory is worse than no memory.

8
00:00:28,846 --> 00:00:35,186
Alex: Welcome to The Daily Signal. Your daily download on A I, growth, and building things.

9
00:00:35,796 --> 00:00:36,964
Alex: I'm Alex.

10
00:00:37,589 --> 00:00:43,164
Maya: And I'm Maya. It's Friday, October sixteenth, twenty twenty-six.

11
00:00:43,615 --> 00:00:47,024
Alex: Quick note first. Today's episode is a little different.

12
00:00:47,024 --> 00:00:52,477
Alex: No music today, just the two of us. And it is a bit shorter than usual.

13
00:00:53,091 --> 00:00:57,279
Maya: Today's notes come from Fisher's X home feed.

14
00:00:57,279 --> 00:01:01,885
Maya: Only the Following feed, read once, early this morning, Beijing time.

15
00:01:01,885 --> 00:01:06,073
Maya: So some posts from yesterday daytime are not in here.

16
00:01:06,682 --> 00:01:12,663
Alex: Here's what's coming today. One. Agent memory goes stale, and what to do about it.

17
00:01:13,206 --> 00:01:17,933
Maya: Two. Using A I to protect the bar, not to make more stuff.

18
00:01:18,444 --> 00:01:22,415
Alex: Three. Why so many A I-built websites look the same.

19
00:01:22,772 --> 00:01:29,435
Maya: 简单说：今天讲记忆过期、守住标准、还有长得一样的网站。

20
00:01:30,435 --> 00:01:32,502
Alex: Segment one. Top stories.

21
00:01:33,002 --> 00:01:35,123
Maya: First post is from klöss.

22
00:01:35,123 --> 00:01:39,719
Maya: That's a builder account on X with about seventy-nine thousand followers.

23
00:01:40,258 --> 00:01:44,491
Alex: His title is: Your agent's memory is lying to you.

24
00:01:45,042 --> 00:01:47,747
Maya: Strong title. What does he mean?

25
00:01:48,307 --> 00:01:54,395
Alex: Memory files and rule files get old. They go stale. But the agent reads them as fact.

26
00:01:54,395 --> 00:01:57,259
Alex: And it acts on them with full confidence.

27
00:01:57,908 --> 00:02:01,685
Maya: So it never says, hmm, maybe this is outdated?

28
00:02:02,293 --> 00:02:06,930
Alex: No. It just does it. His fix is a monthly audit.

29
00:02:06,930 --> 00:02:14,097
Alex: You ask the agent to read every memory and rule file. Then flag three kinds of lines.

30
00:02:14,498 --> 00:02:16,337
Maya: Okay. Which three?

31
00:02:16,985 --> 00:02:21,419
Alex: Lines that contradict another file. Lines that are probably outdated.

32
00:02:21,419 --> 00:02:25,410
Alex: And lines written as fact that you never confirmed.

33
00:02:25,903 --> 00:02:27,246
Maya: And then it fixes them?

34
00:02:27,728 --> 00:02:30,163
Alex: No. That's the key part.

35
00:02:30,163 --> 00:02:36,252
Alex: For each line, it quotes it, explains why it looks wrong, and suggests a fix.

36
00:02:36,252 --> 00:02:39,093
Alex: But it changes nothing until you approve.

37
00:02:39,493 --> 00:02:45,483
Maya: 也就是说：让 A I 找出过期的规则，但你批准前它不能改。

38
00:02:45,942 --> 00:02:51,431
Alex: His last line is the one I like. Stale memory is worse than no memory.

39
00:02:51,431 --> 00:02:54,724
Alex: At least with no memory, the agent asks you.

40
00:02:55,624 --> 00:02:59,429
Maya: And this is not just theory for Fisher. It happened this week.

41
00:02:59,886 --> 00:03:00,364
Alex: Right.

42
00:03:00,364 --> 00:03:06,602
Alex: On October fifteenth, the storage plan for his project changed twice in one morning.

43
00:03:06,602 --> 00:03:11,058
Alex: First, the cloud computer was the one source of truth.

44
00:03:11,058 --> 00:03:15,960
Alex: Then, about forty-five minutes later, it moved to his laptop.

45
00:03:16,512 --> 00:03:19,855
Maya: And the old line is still in the memory file.

46
00:03:19,855 --> 00:03:24,200
Maya: The README also still says the cloud computer is the source of truth.

47
00:03:24,744 --> 00:03:29,182
Alex: So a new assistant could read the old line first and trust it.

48
00:03:29,815 --> 00:03:37,163
Maya: Our daily suggests one thing. Ask the Owner bot to clean out the old storage lines.

49
00:03:37,163 --> 00:03:39,919
Maya: But Fisher decides. Nothing was changed.

50
00:03:40,494 --> 00:03:43,105
Alex: And this is also today's original post idea.

51
00:03:43,105 --> 00:03:46,005
Alex: We'll come back to that in the content segment.

52
00:03:46,557 --> 00:03:50,757
Maya: So the takeaway is: when you change a rule, delete the old line.

53
00:03:50,757 --> 00:03:53,665
Maya: Don't just add a new one below it.

54
00:03:54,914 --> 00:03:58,922
Maya: 一句话：改规矩时，把旧的那行删掉。

55
00:04:01,772 --> 00:04:08,210
Alex: Next, Eric Siu. He runs a marketing agency. About forty-six thousand followers.

56
00:04:08,710 --> 00:04:13,902
Maya: His post says: I use A I to protect the bar more than to produce more.

57
00:04:14,354 --> 00:04:17,334
Alex: Protect the bar. Meaning keep the standard high.

58
00:04:17,822 --> 00:04:23,711
Maya: He gives three bots as examples. First, an outreach bot. It has a daily limit.

59
00:04:23,711 --> 00:04:29,207
Maya: If it can't find enough good people to contact, it sends fewer emails.

60
00:04:29,795 --> 00:04:32,636
Alex: Instead of lowering the standard to hit a number.

61
00:04:33,126 --> 00:04:35,508
Maya: Exactly. Second, a hiring bot.

62
00:04:35,508 --> 00:04:39,796
Maya: It scores every applicant against a frozen role description.

63
00:04:39,796 --> 00:04:45,514
Maya: So the first candidate and the last candidate get the same ruler.

64
00:04:45,882 --> 00:04:46,346
Alex: Third?

65
00:04:46,797 --> 00:04:47,955
Maya: A sponsorship bot.

66
00:04:47,955 --> 00:04:54,516
Maya: When someone asks for a price, the first reply is fixed, and there is a minimum price.

67
00:04:54,516 --> 00:04:56,446
Maya: Exceptions go to a human.

68
00:04:57,062 --> 00:05:02,439
Alex: So a person makes the decision once. Then the bot applies it the same way every day.

69
00:05:02,779 --> 00:05:10,088
Maya: 简单说：人先定好标准，Bot 每天照着执行，不为凑数降低标准。

70
00:05:10,592 --> 00:05:12,955
Alex: He had a second post the same day.

71
00:05:12,955 --> 00:05:17,092
Alex: He says if only you can use a bot, you just made yourself faster.

72
00:05:17,092 --> 00:05:21,525
Alex: If someone else on the team can use it, you change how the department works.

73
00:05:22,151 --> 00:05:28,027
Maya: This matches Fisher's own rules. His home feed is read only once a day.

74
00:05:28,027 --> 00:05:33,512
Maya: Stop near the budget limit. And every draft waits for him to click send.

75
00:05:34,064 --> 00:05:37,706
Alex: Fisher replied to Eric's read-only bot post yesterday.

76
00:05:37,706 --> 00:05:40,984
Alex: So this one is a natural follow-up reply.

77
00:05:41,603 --> 00:05:47,575
Maya: So the takeaway is: most good limits for A I are about doing less, not more.

78
00:05:48,853 --> 00:05:53,076
Maya: 一句话：给 A I 的规矩，大多是让它少做。

79
00:05:55,926 --> 00:05:58,445
Alex: Segment two. Building and design.

80
00:05:59,044 --> 00:06:05,410
Maya: This one is from Shann Holmberg, based in Lisbon. About thirty-seven thousand followers.

81
00:06:05,879 --> 00:06:10,204
Alex: The topic: why so many A I-built websites look the same.

82
00:06:10,763 --> 00:06:16,920
Maya: Because everyone uses the same model. And the model has default design choices.

83
00:06:17,306 --> 00:06:17,860
Alex: Like what?

84
00:06:18,484 --> 00:06:25,159
Maya: The same headline style. The same hover effects. Text that animates word by word.

85
00:06:25,681 --> 00:06:30,420
Alex: Ah. I've seen those. Cards that lift up when your mouse goes over them.

86
00:06:30,989 --> 00:06:37,745
Maya: Yes, and for no reason. So your site starts to look familiar. Not because of your brand.

87
00:06:37,745 --> 00:06:39,732
Maya: Just because of the model.

88
00:06:40,194 --> 00:06:42,631
Alex: Can't you just tell it, don't look like A I?

89
00:06:43,158 --> 00:06:49,707
Maya: He says that's not enough. He points to Anthropic's guide for Opus five point five.

90
00:06:49,707 --> 00:06:54,714
Maya: Saying avoid the generic look can just swap one default style for another.

91
00:06:55,076 --> 00:06:55,975
Alex: So what works?

92
00:06:56,455 --> 00:07:02,009
Maya: Name the exact patterns you don't want. Italic accent words in every headline.

93
00:07:02,009 --> 00:07:06,373
Maya: Cards that lift on hover. Then give reference sites you like.

94
00:07:06,373 --> 00:07:13,911
Maya: And save those decisions in a brand book, so you don't fix the same thing on every project.

95
00:07:14,624 --> 00:07:21,352
Maya: 也就是说：别笼统说“不要 A I 感”，要点名具体不要哪几种样式。

96
00:07:21,878 --> 00:07:23,620
Alex: Why does this matter for Fisher?

97
00:07:24,266 --> 00:07:29,686
Maya: He doesn't write code. His websites are built with A I.

98
00:07:29,686 --> 00:07:32,847
Maya: YouChina, Burn four fifty-one, Aluminium China.

99
00:07:33,415 --> 00:07:35,961
Alex: And he already has the same rule for writing.

100
00:07:36,532 --> 00:07:40,967
Maya: Right. "Don't sound like A I" doesn't work well.

101
00:07:40,967 --> 00:07:46,612
Maya: A list of exact phrases to avoid works better. Same idea, now for design.

102
00:07:47,189 --> 00:07:53,342
Alex: Writing idea? Only if he has real screenshots where his own sites got the same style.

103
00:07:53,342 --> 00:07:55,649
Alex: Without that, the daily says skip.

104
00:07:56,235 --> 00:08:02,852
Maya: So the takeaway is: be specific about what you don't want, and save it so you don't repeat the fix.

105
00:08:04,032 --> 00:08:09,609
Maya: 一句话：把“不要什么”写具体，并存进品牌手册。

106
00:08:12,459 --> 00:08:18,409
Alex: Related post. From an account called staysaasy, about seventeen thousand followers.

107
00:08:18,409 --> 00:08:20,573
Alex: They write about management.

108
00:08:21,111 --> 00:08:25,599
Maya: The story is about interns at small startups in the twenty-tens.

109
00:08:26,097 --> 00:08:28,406
Alex: The summer interns. I remember.

110
00:08:28,937 --> 00:08:34,493
Maya: They got tasks like, make the analytics page cooler. But nobody said what cooler meant.

111
00:08:34,493 --> 00:08:39,309
Maya: Or where the page was. Or how to know when it's done.

112
00:08:39,641 --> 00:08:41,084
Alex: Ha. Classic.

113
00:08:41,728 --> 00:08:46,197
Maya: Their point: a coding agent that nobody guides well is like those interns.

114
00:08:46,197 --> 00:08:49,978
Maya: But they never go home at the end of the summer.

115
00:08:50,326 --> 00:08:51,414
Alex: That's a little scary.

116
00:08:51,998 --> 00:08:55,214
Maya: And all the old ways of managing interns still apply.

117
00:08:55,824 --> 00:09:03,202
Maya: 简单说：管 A I 跟管新人一样，要讲清目标、位置和怎么算做完。

118
00:09:03,827 --> 00:09:12,287
Alex: Fisher managed people for years. His team rules already have handover fields. Goal. Owner.

119
00:09:12,287 --> 00:09:14,703
Alex: Deliverable. Next step. Status.

120
00:09:15,286 --> 00:09:23,485
Maya: So the content idea is: I write tasks for A I the same way I used to write handover notes for new people.

121
00:09:23,485 --> 00:09:24,911
Maya: Good for next week.

122
00:09:25,387 --> 00:09:30,033
Alex: So the takeaway is: a clear handover note works for agents too.

123
00:09:31,062 --> 00:09:35,423
Maya: 一句话：给 A I 写任务，就是写交接单。

124
00:09:38,273 --> 00:09:41,728
Alex: Quick one. Ben Tossell asks a simple question.

125
00:09:41,728 --> 00:09:47,343
Alex: With personal agents, will we end up with one thread, or many agents?

126
00:09:47,982 --> 00:09:51,990
Maya: About two hundred thousand followers. Lots of replies.

127
00:09:52,621 --> 00:09:58,716
Alex: Fisher is in the many-agents group. One for personal brand. One for income opportunities.

128
00:09:58,716 --> 00:10:01,966
Alex: And an Owner bot that checks the others.

129
00:10:02,530 --> 00:10:06,740
Maya: So he can answer from real use. Not just pick a side.

130
00:10:07,219 --> 00:10:11,296
Alex: So the takeaway is: the hard part isn't how many agents.

131
00:10:11,296 --> 00:10:15,373
Alex: It's making sure two of them never edit the same file.

132
00:10:16,318 --> 00:10:22,757
Maya: 一句话：难的不是几个助手，而是别让两个助手改同一份文件。

133
00:10:25,607 --> 00:10:26,437
Maya: Quick break.

134
00:10:26,437 --> 00:10:37,631
Maya: When we come back: an A I tracking problem that fixed itself, a voice tip from Greg Isenberg, and the question of whether websites should become chats.

135
00:10:38,367 --> 00:10:40,862
Maya: 休息一下，马上回来。

136
00:10:42,362 --> 00:10:44,965
Alex: Segment three. Growth and search.

137
00:10:45,526 --> 00:10:50,673
Maya: Lily Ray. She's one of the best-known S E O experts.

138
00:10:50,673 --> 00:10:55,821
Maya: S E O means search engine optimization, how you get found on Google.

139
00:10:56,307 --> 00:10:59,214
Alex: About one hundred twenty-four thousand followers.

140
00:10:59,841 --> 00:11:07,608
Maya: A few days ago, on the October fourteenth daily, we said links from ChatGPT stopped carrying U T M tags.

141
00:11:08,186 --> 00:11:11,856
Alex: U T M tags are just small labels in a link.

142
00:11:11,856 --> 00:11:14,859
Alex: They tell your analytics where a visitor came from.

143
00:11:15,422 --> 00:11:22,073
Maya: Without them, A I traffic can show up as Direct. Like the person typed your address.

144
00:11:22,442 --> 00:11:23,079
Alex: And now?

145
00:11:23,611 --> 00:11:29,770
Maya: Lily Ray says the issue is already patched. She credits another account for the tip.

146
00:11:30,121 --> 00:11:31,889
Alex: Oh. That was fast.

147
00:11:32,347 --> 00:11:40,268
Maya: In a second post, she says Google's new features are things S E O people asked for for years.

148
00:11:40,268 --> 00:11:42,645
Maya: But people still need to click.

149
00:11:42,645 --> 00:11:47,398
Maya: She also wants more exact A I tracking in Google Search Console.

150
00:11:48,123 --> 00:11:53,962
Maya: 也就是说：ChatGPT 链接不带来源标签的问题已经修好了。

151
00:11:54,506 --> 00:12:02,865
Alex: This changes one of Fisher's topic ideas, W M P two b. It was about losing U T M tags.

152
00:12:03,425 --> 00:12:10,727
Maya: So the angle needs to change. Maybe: don't trust only one data source for A I traffic.

153
00:12:10,727 --> 00:12:14,784
Maya: The tags went away and came back in one week.

154
00:12:15,299 --> 00:12:19,253
Alex: His evidence item E V oh two still works.

155
00:12:19,253 --> 00:12:26,723
Alex: Zero ads, and A I assistants are a top source of visits. No absolute numbers in public.

156
00:12:27,323 --> 00:12:31,963
Maya: So the takeaway is: check A I traffic from more than one place.

157
00:12:33,118 --> 00:12:37,442
Maya: 一句话：看 A I 流量别只靠一个口径。

158
00:12:40,292 --> 00:12:47,925
Alex: Brodie Clark, an S E O consultant in Australia. He says rank tracking is broken right now.

159
00:12:48,449 --> 00:12:53,037
Maya: Rank tracking. Checking where your page shows up in Google for a keyword.

160
00:12:53,493 --> 00:12:58,199
Alex: He says poisoned results pages hit almost every data provider.

161
00:12:58,199 --> 00:13:05,259
Alex: His workaround: connect a tool called SERP Lens to Claude Desktop through M C P.

162
00:13:05,734 --> 00:13:09,955
Maya: M C P is a way to plug a tool into an A I assistant.

163
00:13:10,598 --> 00:13:14,859
Alex: Then Claude checks his client's keywords every morning from Sydney.

164
00:13:14,859 --> 00:13:18,345
Alex: And logs the rank and the page into Notion.

165
00:13:18,926 --> 00:13:25,912
Maya: To be fair, the post ends with "comment SERP for the guide". So it's partly marketing.

166
00:13:25,912 --> 00:13:28,789
Maya: And the results are his own claim.

167
00:13:29,325 --> 00:13:35,779
Alex: For Fisher, it's the right style of tool. Plug something in, let A I check every day.

168
00:13:35,779 --> 00:13:38,288
Alex: Maybe try once for YouChina, without paying.

169
00:13:38,843 --> 00:13:44,029
Maya: So the takeaway is: interesting, but not tested by anyone else yet.

170
00:13:45,354 --> 00:13:50,305
Maya: 一句话：可以试，但效果是作者自己说的。

171
00:13:53,155 --> 00:13:59,519
Alex: Greg Isenberg. Big account, about seven hundred thirty-eight thousand followers.

172
00:14:00,038 --> 00:14:06,854
Maya: He posted ten insights from a week with Gemini four Argon. Important: it's sponsored.

173
00:14:06,854 --> 00:14:11,397
Maya: He says Google gave early access and sponsored the episode.

174
00:14:11,965 --> 00:14:14,339
Alex: Good that he says it. Which points matter?

175
00:14:14,926 --> 00:14:19,442
Maya: Two. First, without a reference image, his design looked bad.

176
00:14:19,442 --> 00:14:23,507
Maya: With a Mondrian painting as reference, it looked beautiful.

177
00:14:24,028 --> 00:14:27,027
Alex: References again. Same as the website story.

178
00:14:27,581 --> 00:14:33,307
Maya: Second, he asked it to read everything he ever wrote, then make a voice guide.

179
00:14:33,307 --> 00:14:34,834
Maya: His words, his templates.

180
00:14:35,437 --> 00:14:38,055
Alex: And Fisher does this a bit differently.

181
00:14:38,686 --> 00:14:44,425
Maya: Yes. Fisher's voice source is only his articles from before twenty twenty-five.

182
00:14:44,425 --> 00:14:48,523
Maya: Because newer posts were already written with A I help.

183
00:14:49,003 --> 00:14:52,388
Alex: So if you feed those in, it learns to sound like A I.

184
00:14:53,025 --> 00:14:57,291
Maya: Exactly. That's a detail only someone who did it would know.

185
00:14:57,842 --> 00:15:04,381
Maya: 简单说：让 A I 学口吻时，别给它看已经有 A I 味的新文章。

186
00:15:04,982 --> 00:15:09,746
Alex: So the takeaway is: choose which of your writing the model learns from.

187
00:15:10,772 --> 00:15:14,019
Maya: 一句话：喂给 A I 的样本要挑。

188
00:15:16,869 --> 00:15:23,125
Alex: Now, three posts that fit together. Paul Mit shares Blandie, from a company called Bland.

189
00:15:23,125 --> 00:15:25,627
Alex: You give it any business website.

190
00:15:25,627 --> 00:15:31,882
Alex: It turns it into an A I agent you can text, ask questions, and pay.

191
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:38,628
Maya: No menus, no forms. He asks: is the best interface just a conversation?

192
00:15:39,162 --> 00:15:43,941
Alex: At almost the same time, a Chinese account, 关木, complains about a payment app.

193
00:15:43,941 --> 00:15:46,923
Alex: He just wanted to see his dentist appointment number.

194
00:15:46,923 --> 00:15:50,898
Alex: But the app keeps sending him to an A I chat page.

195
00:15:51,324 --> 00:15:53,237
Maya: Ha. I feel that.

196
00:15:53,690 --> 00:16:00,182
Alex: And Karri Saarinen, the founder of Linear, says more U I is being built than ever.

197
00:16:00,757 --> 00:16:06,080
Maya: So, a growth view. Chat helps people who don't know what they're looking for.

198
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:09,074
Maya: Menus help people who know exactly what they want.

199
00:16:09,682 --> 00:16:13,353
Alex: For a travel site like YouChina, that's real.

200
00:16:13,353 --> 00:16:19,062
Alex: This one is borderline as a writing topic, unless Fisher has a real observation.

201
00:16:19,615 --> 00:16:23,990
Maya: So the takeaway is: don't force everyone into one path.

202
00:16:25,285 --> 00:16:29,268
Maya: 一句话：知道要什么的人，还是需要菜单。

203
00:16:32,118 --> 00:16:34,172
Alex: Segment four. Quick hits.

204
00:16:34,667 --> 00:16:40,503
Maya: Anthropic news. Claude Code Review is now open for Team and Enterprise plans.

205
00:16:40,503 --> 00:16:45,890
Maya: They say most reviews finish about three times faster than in preview.

206
00:16:46,380 --> 00:16:49,049
